---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import PlatformCards from "../components/PlatformCards.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
---

<Layout title="Install" description="How to build and run Tor Browser Installer from source, per-platform defaults, and usage steps.">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">Get started</span>
      <h1>Installation</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="max-width: 62ch;">
        There's no packaged one-click download of Tor Browser Installer <em>itself</em> yet —
        it's beta software, and for now the most trustworthy way to run it is to build it
        yourself from a source tree you can read.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:48px;">1. Clone the repository</h2>
      <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/reality-prod/tbi-rust.git
cd tbi-rust</code></pre>

      <h2 style="margin-top:36px;">2. Install Rust</h2>
      <p>If you don't already have the Rust toolchain, get it from <a href="https://rustup.rs/">rustup.rs</a>.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:36px;">3. Build and run</h2>
      <pre><code>cargo run --release</code></pre>
      <p>Rust compiles one binary per target — there's no single file that runs natively on all three
        operating systems. The same source, built once per OS, resolves the right release JSON,
        archive format, and install routine for whichever platform it was compiled on.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--surface">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">Per-platform defaults</span>
      <h2>Where it installs, and how it earns elevated access.</h2>
      <p class="lede" style="max-width: 62ch; margin-bottom: 32px;">
        Every location below matches that OS's own conventions — the same place Finder,
        your package manager, or a typical installer would put things.
      </p>
      <PlatformCards />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">Using the app</span>
      <h2>Once it's running</h2>
      <ol class="usage-list">
        <li><strong>Launch the installer</strong> — run the compiled binary.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose an install location</strong> — pre-filled with the platform default above, editable in the text field.</li>
        <li><strong>Pick a scope</strong> — <em>Just me</em> (no special privileges) or <em>All users</em> (system-wide, needs a password).</li>
        <li><strong>Click “Download &amp; Install”</strong> — the app shows you the version and URL, waits for your confirmation, then downloads, verifies, and installs.</li>
        <li><strong>Launch Tor Browser</strong> — directly from the completion screen, or from the app's own “already installed” screen next time.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--surface">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">Troubleshooting</span>
      <h2>Common issues</h2>
      <div class="trouble">
        <div>
          <h4><Icon name="cross" size={16} /> “Could not fetch release information”</h4>
          <p>Check your connection, or the Tor Project's release API may be briefly unavailable — try again shortly.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4><Icon name="cross" size={16} /> “Checksum mismatch”</h4>
          <p>The download was corrupted or intercepted in transit. The installer refuses to install it — download again.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4><Icon name="cross" size={16} /> “PGP signature verification failed”</h4>
          <p>The signature doesn't match the Tor Browser Developers key. The installer refuses to install it — this can indicate a compromised download.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4><Icon name="cross" size={16} /> Permission denied (macOS/Linux)</h4>
          <p>System-wide installs need your administrator password for <code>sudo -s</code>. It's used locally for that one command and is never stored or transmitted.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:28px;">Click <strong>View commands</strong> inside the app at any time to see every system command it has run, in order — useful for debugging, and for checking its work.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .narrow { max-width: 760px; }
  .usage-list { display: grid; gap: 14px; padding-left: 22px; }
  .usage-list li { color: var(--text); }
  .trouble { display: grid; gap: 24px; margin-top: 24px; }
  .trouble h4 { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; color: var(--app-error); margin-bottom: 6px; }
  .trouble p { margin: 0; color: var(--text-muted); }
</style>
